  "statement": "The Accelerationist raises an important point regarding modular software development. Developers build foundation models as general tools, while downstream deployers best understand domain-specific operational risks. Under the Restatement (Third) of Torts § 5, component manufacturers avoid liability unless they substantially participate in integrating a defective final product. This legal shield fails because generative foundation models are not inert components like sheet metal or basic microprocessors. They actively generate execution logic and direct autonomous workflows across non-linear environments. My argument rests on one clear assumption: software agents will increasingly execute high-stakes transactions without direct human step-by-step verification. Omitting an architectural safety supervisor leaves downstream deployers defenceless against machine-speed hallucinations and cascading errors.\n\nSystems safety engineering demonstrates that passive post-hoc telemetry cannot prevent catastrophic failures. In 1993, the Federal Aviation Administration mandated Traffic Alert and Collision Avoidance Systems across commercial aviation. TCAS functions as an independent, deterministic interlock that directly overrides pilot controls during impending collisions. Similarly, the IEC 61508 industrial standard requires independent safety instrumented systems physically separated from the primary process controller. Autonomous AI systems deployed into high-liability enterprise environments require equivalent technical guardrails paired with deterministic human shutdown capacity.\n\nUnder the Restatement (Third) of Torts § 2(b), a product is defectively designed when a reasonable alternative design could have reduced foreseeable risks. The dual-layered safety supervisor function is that reasonable alternative design. It combines documented technical filtering for known failure modes with institutional rollback authority for novel hazards. When Boeing integrated the Maneuvering Characteristics Augmentation System into the 737 MAX without redundant sensor interlocks, 346 people died. Exempting upstream model developers from design defect liability allows them to capture commercial returns while externalizing catastrophic systemic risks onto the public.\n\nThe Federal Trade Commission and sector regulators must treat the absence of supervisory interlocks as an actionable design defect. Enterprise insurers and risk officers support clear design standards because ambiguous upstream liability leaves downstream businesses holding uninsurable losses. Upstream foundation model developers will oppose mandatory safety supervisors to avoid shared liability with commercial deployers. We spent a century building institutional guardrails to stabilize civil aviation, nuclear power, and medicine. On what authority do we claim we can deploy autonomous intelligence without them?",
