=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [skp-intentions-141] Regulate AI for Documented Harms, Not Science-Fiction Scenarios: False Dilemma — The node frames the choice as a binary between regulating present harms and addressing speculative risks, when proportional parallel resource allocation across both horizons is a viable and commonly practiced approach in other regulatory domains.
- [skp-beliefs-104] Market-Access Leverage Allows Rich Nations to Dictate AI Rules to Weaker Economies: Intentionality Fallacy (Structural vs. Deliberate) — The node frames market-access leverage as deliberate regulatory 'dictation,' but much of the Brussels Effect operates through autonomous corporate compliance decisions rather than direct coercion by states, conflating structural outcomes with intentional imposition.
- [skp-beliefs-118] AI Creates Economically Productive but Socially Harmful Outputs: Ecological fallacy / attribution error — Societal harms from addictive platforms or manipulative ads are attributed to 'AI outputs' specifically, when the same harms substantially predate AI systems and stem from underlying business model incentives. Conflating the platform incentive problem with an AI-specific problem weakens the causal chain.
- [skp-beliefs-188] Layering Multiple AI Legislative Acts Creates Coordination Failures: Questionable Cause (Post hoc / Correlation-Causation) — The claim asserts a causal link between legislative layering and coordination failures, but observed failures in multi-jurisdictional AI governance may stem from political will deficits, under-resourced agencies, or technical complexity rather than the layering itself.
- [skp-desires-075] Democratic Accountability in AI Development: False Dilemma — The node frames AI governance as a binary choice between private profit and democratic accountability, obscuring viable hybrid models (e.g., regulated markets, public-benefit corporations, co-regulatory schemes) that blend market efficiency with public interest constraints.
- [skp-beliefs-138] Profit-Driven Algorithmic Nudging Undermines Fiduciary Duty: Motive attribution fallacy — The framing assumes that because financial advisers have profit incentives and access to behavioral analytics, they are therefore using these tools against client interests — conflating capability and motive with demonstrated harmful action.
- [skp-beliefs-113] Institutional Inertia From Entrenched Lobbies Prevents Regulatory Frameworks From Evolving: Survivorship bias — The claim foregrounds visible cases where entrenched lobbies blocked beneficial regulatory adaptation while systematically ignoring counterfactuals where stable regulatory frameworks prevented harmful market failures, crises, or negative externalities. The absence of disasters attributable to regulatory stability is structurally invisible.
- [skp-intentions-079] Mandate Federal Agency Reporting to Congress on AI Impacts to Vulnerable Communities: Conflation of monitoring with protection — The node treats the act of reporting AI impacts to Congress as equivalent to protecting vulnerable communities, when reporting alone does not compel remediation or halt harmful deployments.
- [skp-intentions-097] Establish Independent Lifecycle Oversight Mechanisms for AI Systems: Appeal to Regulability — The node assumes that because other industries (finance, environment) have been regulated, AI can be similarly governed — without adequately addressing AI's distinctive opacity, rapid iteration cycles, and the difficulty of defining 'compliance' for probabilistic outputs.
- [skp-desires-070] Vigilant Pragmatism Toward AI: False Balance (Golden Mean Fallacy) — The node implicitly treats a midpoint between optimism and pessimism as epistemically superior, when the correct posture may be closer to one pole depending on context and evidence.

