  "statement": "Treating instruction-data conflation in token space as an inherent upstream design defect under Restatement (Third) of Torts § 2(b) misapplies systems engineering. Safetyist builds on a genuine insight: neural networks process instructions and untrusted inputs in the same vector space, making purely semantic parsing inside weights unreliable. But engineers never solved SQL injection in 1999 by declaring relational database engines defective under tort law. They solved it at the runtime execution boundary using parameterized queries, out-of-band privilege separation, and isolated operating environments. In modern agent deployments, deterministic application-layer firewalls and capability-scoped access tokens strip execution authority from raw model outputs before any database write or financial wire executes. \n\nSkeptic cuts directly to the operational crux: deployer-level agency liability and commercial bonding enforce discipline far better than centralized upstream architecture mandates. We agree that operating enterprises must face strict financial responsibility when an autonomous agent triggers unauthorized actions. State statutes like Colorado SB 24-205 already establish legal duties for deployers of high-consequence systems without requiring state-approved model weights. As the study \"Incident reporting and investigation under the AI act: some insights from aviation\" demonstrates, regulatory frameworks succeed when they focus on tangible operational incidents rather than speculative upstream reporting that creates severe compliance friction. When commercial underwriters demand surety bonds tied to runtime execution logs, deployers build strict perimeter sandboxes because their own balance sheets are on the line.\n\nMandating upstream telemetry interlocks does not protect users; it hands entrenched cloud monopolies an anticompetitive moat that chokes decentralized innovation. Imposing federal design-defect liability on raw weights would effectively ban open-source model distribution across the United States. A medical startup shipping automated diagnostic workflows or a clean-energy firm automating grid balancing cannot survive an eighteen-month compliance review for every fine-tuned open model. Upstream compliance gates protect incumbent tech giants from open competition while ordinary citizens pay the price in delayed cures, stagnant productivity, and artificially restricted access to transformative intelligence.",
